  "meta": {
    "title": "The Pearls",
    "language": "en",
    "category": "P1"
  },
  "passage": {
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "id": "passage-heading",
        "level": 2,
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "id": "passage-heading-text",
            "text": "The Pearls"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "id": "passage-paragraph-A",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "id": "passage-paragraph-A-text",
            "text": "Pearls are formed inside the shells of certain molluscs. When a small irritant such as a grain of sand lodges in the soft tissue, the animal coats it with layers of a substance called nacre. Over several years these layers build up into the hard, lustrous object that jewellers value."
          }
        ],
        "paragraphLabel": "A"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "id": "passage-paragraph-B",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "id": "passage-paragraph-B-text",
            "text": "For most of history pearls were harvested by divers who prised open wild oysters by hand. The work was dangerous and the yield unpredictable, so a single large pearl could cost as much as a ship. Roman writers complained that the fashion for pearl jewellery drained the empire of silver."
          }
        ],
        "paragraphLabel": "B"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "id": "passage-paragraph-C",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "id": "passage-paragraph-C-text",
            "text": "In the late nineteenth century Japanese researchers learned to insert a small bead of mother-of-pearl into an oyster and let the animal coat it. Cultured pearls made the gem ordinary: by the 1930s a respectable necklace cost a month's wages rather than a fortune."
          }
        ],
        "paragraphLabel": "C"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "id": "passage-paragraph-D",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "id": "passage-paragraph-D-text",
            "text": "Modern farms now monitor water temperature, salinity and food supply to keep their oysters healthy. Some producers bleach or dye the harvest, a practice that buyers' associations have tried to regulate, with limited success."
          }
        ],
        "paragraphLabel": "D"
      }
    ],
    "paragraphMap": {
      "A": "passage-paragraph-A",
      "B": "passage-paragraph-B",
      "C": "passage-paragraph-C",
      "D": "passage-paragraph-D"
    }
  },
